  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on parent-reported mobility licenses as a proxy for actual independent mobility behaviors during the pandemic.",
      "what": "This longitudinal study tracked changes in children's independent mobility by surveying 2,291 Canadian parents of 7- to 12-year-olds every six months between December 2020 and December 2021. While average independent mobility licenses increased by 0.65 during the pandemic, linear regression models revealed that boys experienced a smaller increase than girls (beta: -0.60). Parental risk tolerance was positively associated with mobility increases, whereas parental crime safety concerns and non-full-time parental employment were negatively associated.",
      "why": "It provides rare longitudinal evidence on how children's independent mobility shifts over time, demonstrating that gender, parental risk tolerance, and employment status interact to shape how children regain outdoor freedom during a public health crisis."
    }
  },
